  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 234 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Tranquil weather is expected to continue tonight into \ntomorrow with high pressure in place overhead. Skies will remain \nclear and dry this evening into tonight with lows overnight in the \nupper 50s to low 60s. A few more protected valleys in the \nAdirondacks and NEK could see some lower 50s tonight with better \nradiational cooling. Tomorrow will be equally as pleasant as today, \nthough with slightly more terrain driven fair weather cumulus. Our \narea of high pressure will shift a little south and east allowing \nfor better southerly flow and moisture advection. Temperatures will \nbe a degree or two warmer than today in the mid to low to mid 80s. \nDewpoints will also be on the increase around 60. In the decoupled \nvalleys tonight some patchy fog may be possible.\n\nOur calm pattern will erode by Thursday. A Bermuda high responsible \nfor our calm weather today will begin to shift slightly to the \nsouth with the return of troughing. Broad cyclonic flow by the \nend of the week will allow for better moisture advection into \nthe region with a few shortwaves moving through. Temperatures \nThursday will rise into the mid to upper 80s with dewpoints \naround 70 degrees. Heat indices will rise into the upper 80s to \naround 90 as a result. There does not look to be any real \ndefined boundary for shower activity, but shortwaves Thursday \nand Friday look to bring enough energy to support shower and \nthunderstorm chances both afternoons. CAMs denote terrain \ninstability around 1000-1500J/kg with around 20-30 knots of \nshear. CAPE and shear profiles will be supportive of a few \nthunderstorms across the Adirondacks. Should ingredients align \nThursday, there is a chance for some stronger storms, primarily \nin northern New York, given good instability. Weaker shear \nprofiles do lend some uncertainty to the strength of any storms \nin addition to weaker mid level lapse rates. By Friday, \nsouthwesterly flow and shear will increase to 30-40knots with \nsimilar instability levels, expanding into the northern \nChamplain Valley as well. Afternoon scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are anticipated, mainly over the terrain. A few \nstronger storms Friday may also be possible, especially with \nbetter shear and mid level lapse rates. Any storms will wane \nwith the loss of diurnal heating in the evenings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Bermuda high remains in place through the entirety of \nthe extended portion of our forecast from Friday night through \nTuesday. This will mean warm temperatures and active weather for \nnorthern New York and Vermont. Current model predictions are for \nSaturday to be the wettest day of the period, and Sunday to be the \nwarmest. Tough to time fine scale features this far out without a \nsignificant synoptic feature. But, there are indications that upper \nlevel troughs and shortwave energy passing through will bring \nchances for showers more days than not. Looking further out we could \nhave a pattern change towards the end of next week. Highs in the 80s \nand lows in the 60s for the extended period.",
